items.design:
1. ds/eslint-plugin/classes.js, where classesOf moves from index.js:159-180, exports classify(parsed) → {owner, cause, data}, memoised per class string. The owner is the first match in this order:
- no-margin, for any margin, stock or token, arbitrary or not;
- no-dark-variant;
- no-unknown-variant, once it lands;
- no-arbitrary-value;
- no-alpha-token, only when the base without /N is a token class;
- no-static-design-value;
- no-deprecated-token;
- no-non-token-class.
The principle: the owner is the rule whose fix removes or rewrites the whole class, and a rule whose fix would leave the class broken never owns it. The miners proposed three different orders. This one keeps closure: no-arbitrary-value would otherwise suggest mt-150 for mt-[13px]. And stories turn no-arbitrary-value off (index.js:1165-1172) while no-margin stays on, so mt-[13px] in a story is still reported.
2. no-non-token-class gets causes, each its own messageId: 'palette-alpha' (bg-red-500/50), 'runtime' (from site-precision) and 'unknown'. 'stock' and 'typo' arrive with nearest-token, and 'vocabulary' with vocabulary-aliases.
3. Each token rule reports only when classify(c).owner is its own id, which replaces the skip at index.js:801. Two known limits are pinned by tests: bleed.tsx turns no-margin off (index.js:1160-1163), and an arbitrary value in a story is silent by design.
4. ds/test/lint-ownership.test.mjs:
- (a) over the stock corpus, the count of tokens with two or more findings is 0, or the corpus baseline's doubleReported while it ratchets down;
- (b) expectIndependentRules over 10 mixed sources: all rules together give the sorted union of each rule alone, in forward and reversed order. This also guards the shared cache.
